   "text": "What it is Fiat money is currency that a government declares legal tender and that can't be exchanged for a fixed amount of anything else. Nearly every national currency today is fiat. Why people hold it Fiat isn't \"backed\" by a commodity, but it isn't backed by nothing either. Its demand comes from: Taxes. The state requires taxes to be paid in its own currency. Debts. Loans, mortgages and contracts are written in it, so borrowers must get it to repay. Prices and wages. It is the unit everyone keeps score in. The central bank and the banking system standing behind it. See central bank money. Two different \"cash\" holdings Cash straddles the line on the Cartography of Wealth: Physical notes are bearer and private ( Tier 2 ), but exposed to theft and loss. Money in the bank is a bank deposit: recorded, legible and protected by the state ( Tier 3 ), but exposed to bank failure, freezes and capital controls. What goes wrong Inflation steadily reduces what each unit buys. After inflation, cash earning little or no interest loses value year after year. See inflation. Currency crises. When trust in a government breaks, its currency can lose most of its value quickly. Capital controls can limit how much you withdraw or move abroad. Key rule: cash is the best tool for spending and short term safety, and one of the worst for storing wealth over decades. Check yourself Q: Why isn't the US dollar in the same position as a memecoin, when neither is backed by anything? A: Because the dollar has forced demand (taxes, debts and global trade are written in it) and a central bank behind it. A memecoin has none of these. See hierarchy of money.",
