   "adv": false,
   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p>The money a central bank issues. It comes in two forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Banknotes</strong> — the physical cash the public can hold.</li>\n<li><strong>Reserves</strong> — balances that commercial banks keep in accounts at the central bank. Access is limited, mainly to banks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both are the <strong>central bank's liability</strong>. Together they are often called the <em>monetary base</em>.</p>\n<h2>Why it exists</h2>\n<p>Banks need a shared, final way to settle with each other. When your bank pays another bank, the final settlement is a transfer of reserves on the central bank's books. Everything above it — <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bank-deposits\" data-page=\"bank-deposits\">Bank Deposits</a>, <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/eurodollars\" data-page=\"eurodollars\" data-adv=\"1\">Eurodollars</a>, <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/stablecoins\" data-page=\"stablecoins\">Stablecoins</a> — is ultimately a promise to pay in central bank money.</p>\n<h2>How it works</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Creation.</strong> The central bank creates reserves when it buys assets (usually government <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bonds\" data-page=\"bonds\">Bonds</a>) or lends to banks. It removes them when it sells assets or loans are repaid.</li>\n<li><strong>Policy rates.</strong> The central bank sets the interest rate it pays on reserves and steers the rate banks charge each other for overnight loans. That rate ripples out into other interest rates.</li>\n<li><strong>Balance sheet size.</strong> Large asset purchases (\"quantitative easing\") expand reserves; letting assets run off (\"quantitative tightening\") shrinks them. This feeds directly into <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/liquidity\" data-page=\"liquidity\">Liquidity</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What goes wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inflation</strong> if money is created faster than the economy can absorb it. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/inflation\" data-page=\"inflation\">Inflation</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Loss of trust</strong> in the central bank or government, which pushes people towards assets lower in the <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/hierarchy-of-money\" data-page=\"hierarchy-of-money\">hierarchy of money</a>, such as <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/gold\" data-page=\"gold\">Gold</a>, or towards foreign currencies.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> central bank money is the base layer for almost everything you will hold, which is why central bank decisions move every market at once.</p>\n</blockquote>",
