   "text": "What it is A way of ranking forms of money by how final they are . Each layer is a promise to pay in the layer beneath it. Layer Example Whose promise? Base gold Nobody's — final in itself Central bank money Notes, bank reserves (central bank money) The central bank's Bank money bank deposits A commercial bank's, payable in central bank money Offshore and private dollars eurodollars, stablecoins A foreign bank or a company, payable in dollars Securities t bills, bonds A borrower's, payable in future money Risk assets shares, real estate, crypto Priced in money, but not a promise of money The economist Perry Mehrling describes the modern system this way. The investor John Exter drew it as an inverted pyramid : a small base of gold at the bottom, with ever larger layers of credit stacked on top. Why it matters In good times the layers look interchangeable. A deposit, a stablecoin and a T bill all feel like \"a dollar\". In a crisis people sell the upper layers to get the lower ones. Bank runs, stablecoin de pegs and \"dash for cash\" episodes are all moves down the pyramid. Elasticity at the top, discipline at the base. Upper layers can expand quickly through lending; the base is scarce. That is why credit booms and busts happen in the upper layers. Where the US dollar sits The dollar and a memecoin are both \"fiat\" in the narrow sense: neither can be redeemed for anything. But they sit in very different places: Dollars are the central bank's liability , sit near the base of the global hierarchy, and have forced demand : US taxes must be paid in them, and trillions of dollars of debt worldwide must be repaid in them. A memecoin is nobody's liability , has no cash flows, no tax demand and no lender of last resort. Its value rests on attention alone. So the useful question is not \"is it backed?\" but \"how far from the base is it, and what forces people to hold it?\" See claims and liabilities. Key rule: the lower the layer, the fewer things can go wrong with it — but the less it tends to pay. Check yourself Q: A stablecoin trades below one dollar during a market panic. In hierarchy terms, what is happening? A: Holders doubt the issuer can pay one dollar per coin, so they sell the private promise (upper layer) to get dollars (lower layer). The price gap is the market's discount on that promise.",
